Control Flow Statements in Java
Control flow statements determine the order in which program statements are executed. They allow a program to make decisions, repeat tasks, or jump to another part of the code.

Types of Control Flow Statements
1. Decision-Making Statements
These statements execute different blocks of code based on a condition.

Types:

if – Executes a block if the condition is true.
if-else – Executes one block if the condition is true, otherwise executes another block.
else-if ladder – Checks multiple conditions one by one.
switch – Selects one block of code from multiple options based on an expression.
Example:

int age = 18;

if (age >= 18) {
    System.out.println("Eligible to vote");
} else {
    System.out.println("Not eligible");
}

2. Looping (Iteration) Statements
These statements execute a block of code repeatedly until a condition becomes false.

Types:

for loop – Used when the number of iterations is known.
while loop – Executes while the condition is true.
do-while loop – Executes at least once, then repeats while the condition is true.
Example:

for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}

3. Jump (Branching) Statements
These statements transfer control from one part of the program to another.

Types:

break – Terminates a loop or switch statement.
continue – Skips the current iteration and moves to the next iteration of the loop.
return – Exits from a method and optionally returns a value.
Example:

for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3)
        break;
    System.out.println(i);
}

Output:

1
2

Summary
Type	Statements	Purpose
Decision-Making	if, if-else, else-if, switch	Makes decisions based on conditions
Looping	for, while, do-while	Repeats a block of code
Jump	break, continue, return	Changes the normal flow of execution

Definition for exams:
Control flow statements are statements that control the order in which a program's instructions are executed. They are classified into decision-making statements, looping statements, and jump statements.


